  "cues": [
   [
    0.104,
    "Now we zoom into the nucleus."
   ],
   [
    2.24,
    "It is usually the largest organelle, and it holds most of the cell's DNA, and most of its genes."
   ],
   [
    8.62,
    "Most, not all, because mitochondria and chloroplasts carry a little DNA of their own."
   ],
   [
    14.375,
    "The nucleus is wrapped in the nuclear envelope, a double membrane."
   ],
   [
    18.411,
    "Each membrane is a lipid bilayer, and together they separate the nucleus from the cytoplasm."
   ],
   [
    24.245,
    "The envelope is dotted with nuclear pores."
   ],
   [
    27.057,
    "Each pore is lined with a protein structure called a pore complex, and it regulates which molecules go in and out."
   ],
   [
    33.971,
    "Remember the messenger RNA leaving in chapter five?"
   ],
   [
    37.253,
    "This is the gate it used."
   ],
   [
    38.802,
    "Inside the envelope, a net of protein called the nuclear lamina holds the nucleus in shape."
   ],
   [
    44.87,
    "And as drawn here, the outer membrane runs straight into the rough endoplasmic reticulum."
   ]
  ]
